/* KNGS SLR 1–3 revision content: original examples, no assessment-question reuse. */
const SLR13_DATA={"topics":[{"id":"components","slr":1,"ref":"1.1.1(a)","title":"Inside the CPU","desc":"ALU, control unit, five registers, buses and assembly connections","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr01-alu-cu-registers-and-buses/","html":"\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>central processing unit (CPU)\u003c/strong> interprets and executes instructions. It contains several cooperating components rather than being one single calculator. This topic links CPU hardware directly to the execution of low-level programmes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU)\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Performs arithmetic, comparisons, Boolean logic and bit shifting. Results may be held temporarily in the accumulator or another register. The ALU does not independently decide which instruction should run next.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Control Unit (CU)\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Coordinates fetch, decode and execute operations. It sends control signals, manages data movement and interprets opcodes. It does not perform the main arithmetic calculations itself.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>The five named registers\u003c/h3>\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Register\u003c/th>\u003cth>Full name\u003c/th>\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>PC\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Programme Counter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stores the address of the next instruction to fetch. Normally increments during fetching; a branch can change its value.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>MAR\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Memory Address Register\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Holds the address of the memory location that the CPU wants to read or write.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>MDR\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Memory Data Register\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Holds data or instructions moving between main memory and the CPU.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>CIR\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Current Instruction Register\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Holds the fetched instruction while it is decoded and executed.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>ACC\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Accumulator\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Temporarily holds arithmetic/logic results in a simplified accumulator architecture.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Registers versus RAM:\u003c/b> registers are tiny, high-speed CPU storage locations. RAM is larger main memory that stores running instructions and working data. The CPU uses buses to access it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Three system buses\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Address bus\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Identifies a memory or device address, normally from CPU to memory. Its width determines the number of distinct addressable locations: \u003ccode>2\u003csup>n\u003c/sup>\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> address lines.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Data bus\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Carries data and fetched instructions in both directions between processor, memory and devices. Wider transfers can move more bits in one operation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Control bus\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Carries timing, read/write and other control signals, some towards and some from the CPU.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Word size and addressability\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Word length is the natural data size operated on by the machine. Address-bus width and memory addressability together determine the maximum directly addressable capacity; do not assume an address always refers to one byte.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Fresh worked calculation\u003c/b>\u003cp>A simple teaching machine has a 10-line address bus and each address selects a 16-bit word. It can identify \u003cb>2\u003csup>10\u003c/sup> = 1,024 words\u003c/b>, giving \u003cb>1,024 × 2 = 2,048 bytes\u003c/b> of addressed storage. If it were byte-addressable instead, the same address width would identify 1,024 bytes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Connecting assembly to registers\u003c/h3>\u003cp>An instruction such as \u003ccode>LOAD 40\u003c/code> in a simplified direct-addressing processor makes the CU request data from memory location 40. The address passes via MAR and the address bus; the fetched value passes through MDR and the data bus, before reaching a working register. \u003ccode>ADD 41\u003c/code> can then combine the next value using the ALU. Specific machine instruction sets differ.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Common misconception\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The PC does \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> store the instruction itself. The CIR stores the current instruction; MAR stores an address and MDR stores the value or instruction transferred to/from that address.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>"},{"id":"fde","slr":1,"ref":"1.1.1(b)","title":"Fetch–decode–execute cycle","desc":"Step-by-step changes to PC, MAR, MDR, CIR and ACC","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr01-fetch-decode-execute-cycle/","html":"\n\u003cp>The fetch–decode–execute (FDE) cycle repeatedly obtains an instruction from memory, identifies what it means and carries it out. Understanding \u003cstrong>register changes\u003c/strong>, not just the stage names, is essential.\u003c/p>\n\u003col class=\"steps\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Fetch 1:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>MAR ← PC\u003c/code>. The address of the next instruction is placed in MAR and on the address bus.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cb>Fetch 2:\u003c/b> CU sends a \u003cem>memory read\u003c/em> control signal. The instruction at the selected memory address is returned on the data bus into \u003ccode>MDR\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cb>Fetch 3:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>PC ← PC + 1\u003c/code> so it normally points to the next sequential instruction.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cb>Fetch 4:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>CIR ← MDR\u003c/code>. The instruction is now available for decoding.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cb>Decode:\u003c/b> CU decodes the opcode and interprets the operand. If extra data is needed it may perform another memory access through MAR and MDR.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cb>Execute:\u003c/b> perform the operation. The ALU may update ACC, a value may be stored, or a branch may change PC.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Fresh original programme\u003c/b>\u003cp>Assume a teaching processor has a programme starting at address 60 with \u003ccode>LOAD 90\u003c/code>, followed by \u003ccode>ADD 91\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>STOP\u003c/code>. Data locations 90 and 91 contain 7 and 5.\u003c/p>\u003cp>First fetch: PC 60 → MAR 60 → MDR \u003ccode>LOAD 90\u003c/code> → PC 61 → CIR \u003ccode>LOAD 90\u003c/code>. Execution then reads memory address 90 into ACC, giving 7. The second instruction adds the value at 91, so ACC becomes \u003cb>12\u003c/b>. Use the interactive FDE trainer to inspect each state change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Opcode\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The instruction field identifying an operation, such as LOAD, ADD or JUMP.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Operand\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The value or address operated on. Interpretation depends on the processor instruction set and addressing mode.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Branches:\u003c/strong> the PC normally increments during fetch, but a taken branch overrides its value during execute. The next fetch then uses the branch target. The order of micro-operations is presented as an educational model; real CPUs can overlap and optimise operations.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"performance","slr":1,"ref":"1.1.1(c)","title":"CPU performance","desc":"Clock speed, number of cores, cache and bus widths","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr01-performance-of-the-cpu/","html":"\n\u003cp>CPU performance depends on the processor \u003cb>clock speed\u003c/b>, available \u003cb>cores\u003c/b>, cache size and organisation, and on how the software uses the hardware. One characteristic alone cannot reliably predict overall performance.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Clock speed\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Measured in Hertz (Hz), typically GHz, giving cycles per second. For a comparable architecture and workload, a higher clock rate can execute more work per second. Instructions may require multiple cycles, so \u003cb>GHz is not instructions per second\u003c/b>. Increased frequency can increase power use and heat.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Multiple cores\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Each CPU core can execute its own instructions. Independent tasks or parallelisable parts of one programme can run simultaneously. Sequential tasks cannot simply be accelerated by adding cores. Scheduling, communication and synchronisation add overhead.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Cache memory\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Small, fast memory close to the CPU holding recently or frequently used instructions and data. A \u003cb>cache hit\u003c/b> reduces the need to access slower RAM; a \u003cb>cache miss\u003c/b> requires data from another level. L1 is typically smallest and fastest, L2 larger, L3 often larger/shared.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Bus width\u003c/h3>\u003cp>An \u003cb>n-bit address bus\u003c/b> distinguishes \u003cb>2\u003csup>n\u003c/sup> addresses\u003c/b>. A wider data bus can transfer more bits per memory operation. Throughput also depends on other characteristics such as timing and memory technology.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Original worked comparison\u003c/b>\u003cp>Two processors share the same architecture and workload. One runs at 2.4 GHz and another at 3.0 GHz. The second provides \u003cb>25% more clock cycles per second\u003c/b>, but the actual speed improvement might be less if performance is limited by memory, caches, I/O, power or heat.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Why more cores may not mean more speed\u003c/h3>\u003cp>If 70% of a task can be parallelised and 30% must run sequentially, the theoretical speed-up with four cores is \u003ccode>1 / (0.30 + 0.70/4)\u003c/code>, about \u003cb>2.11×\u003c/b> rather than 4×. This \u003cem>Amdahl's law\u003c/em> calculation is useful enrichment rather than a named requirement of 1.1.1(c).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Exam guidance\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Describe the mechanism, not just “more is better”. E.g. larger cache can reduce slower RAM accesses when the needed data is cached, but may cost more and may not help a workload with poor locality.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>"},{"id":"pipeline","slr":1,"ref":"1.1.1(d)","title":"Pipelining","desc":"Overlapping fetch, decode and execute stages","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr01-pipelining/","html":"\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Pipelining\u003c/b> improves processor throughput by overlapping stages of multiple instructions. It is like an assembly line: while one instruction executes, another decodes and a third is fetched.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Without pipelining\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A three-stage teaching model completes one instruction every three cycles. Five instructions need 15 cycles if stages are not overlapped.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>With pipelining\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Once the three-stage pipeline is full, an instruction can complete each cycle. Five instructions take 3 + (5 − 1) = \u003cb>7 cycles\u003c/b> in an ideal pipeline.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Throughput versus latency:\u003c/b> pipelining increases completed instructions per unit time, but a single instruction still has to pass through its stages. It does not necessarily reduce the latency of one instruction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Why the ideal model is not always achieved\u003c/h3>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Data hazards:\u003c/strong> an instruction waits for a value produced by an earlier instruction.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Control hazards:\u003c/strong> a branch changes which instruction should execute next; fetched instructions may need discarding.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Structural hazards:\u003c/strong> two overlapping operations may need the same hardware resource.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Uneven stages:\u003c/strong> the slowest stage limits the effective pipeline rate.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Fresh worked example\u003c/b>\u003cp>A stage takes one clock cycle. To complete eight independent instructions with F, D and E stages, ideal non-pipelined execution takes \u003cb>24 cycles\u003c/b>. Ideal pipelined execution takes \u003cb>10 cycles\u003c/b>. The interactive timeline demonstrates how the savings arise.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Modern CPUs may have more than three pipeline stages. The three-stage model is a simplification to teach the principle.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"architectures","slr":1,"ref":"1.1.1(e)","title":"Processor architectures","desc":"Von Neumann, Harvard and contemporary hybrids","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr01-von-neumann-and-harvard/","html":"\n\u003ch3>Stored-program concept\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Programmes are held in memory as instructions, and the CPU fetches, decodes and executes them. Branch instructions can alter the next instruction address.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Feature\u003c/th>\u003cth>Von Neumann\u003c/th>\u003cth>Harvard\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Instructions and data share one address space/memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Separate instruction and data memories/address spaces\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Paths\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Shared path can cause contention\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Separate paths can fetch instruction and data at the same time\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Strengths\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Flexible allocation of memory; simpler general-purpose design\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Separate buses and memories can improve bandwidth\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Trade-offs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Von Neumann bottleneck\u003c/b>: instruction/data transfers compete\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Additional hardware complexity; memory allocations can be less flexible\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Examples\u003c/td>\u003ctd>General-purpose stored-program model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Microcontrollers and digital signal processing systems\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch3>Contemporary architectures\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Many modern processors combine both designs. They may share main memory for code and data (Von Neumann-like) while keeping \u003cb>separate instruction and data L1 caches\u003c/b> (Harvard-like), allowing simultaneous cache access. The terms describe memory organisation, not a simple “old versus new” choice.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Original example\u003c/b>\u003cp>A small audio-processing device needs a steady stream of instructions and sound samples. Separate instruction/data paths can avoid contention in real-time processing. A general-purpose laptop instead benefits from a flexible unified main memory with split L1 caches.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>What to compare in longer answers\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Mention shared or separate memories, whether instruction and data accesses contend, complexity, flexibility and a suitable application. State that real processors commonly use hybrid designs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>"},{"id":"cisc","slr":2,"ref":"1.1.2(a)","title":"CISC and RISC","desc":"Instruction sets, code density, registers and applications","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr02-cisc-vs-risc/","html":"\n\u003cp>CPU architectures differ in their \u003cb>instruction set architecture (ISA)\u003c/b>, the instructions visible to a programmer or compiler. CISC and RISC are contrasting design philosophies; modern real-world designs blend features.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Feature\u003c/th>\u003cth>CISC\u003c/th>\u003cth>RISC\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Name\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Complex Instruction Set Computer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reduced Instruction Set Computer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Instruction set\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Typically many and sometimes complex instructions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Typically simpler, regular instructions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Work per instruction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Some instructions combine several operations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often a load/store design with arithmetic on registers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Encoding\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often variable-length instructions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often fixed-length, regular encoding\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Benefits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Potentially better code density and backward compatibility\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can simplify decoding, pipelining and efficient implementations\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Trade-offs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Complex decoding and microarchitectural design\u003c/td>\u003ctd>May require more instructions for certain tasks\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Fresh conceptual assembly comparison\u003c/b>\u003cp>A hypothetical complex instruction \u003ccode>ADD_MEMORY A, B\u003c/code> might load and add a value at B directly into memory A. A load/store instruction set might instead use \u003ccode>LOAD R1, A\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LOAD R2, B\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ADD R1, R1, R2\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>STORE A, R1\u003c/code>. These are illustrative \u003cem>pseudo-instructions\u003c/em>, not code for a named processor.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Use cases and modern qualifications\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The x86 family is traditionally classed as CISC; ARM families often illustrate RISC design. However, modern CISC processors may translate complex instructions internally into simpler micro-operations, and many RISC processors have advanced instructions. It is \u003cstrong>not correct\u003c/strong> that every RISC instruction always takes one clock cycle or that one approach is universally faster.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"gpu","slr":2,"ref":"1.1.2(b)","title":"GPUs and their uses","desc":"Massively parallel processing beyond graphics","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr02-gpus-and-their-uses/","html":"\n\u003cp>A \u003cb>graphics processing unit (GPU)\u003c/b> contains many processing units designed to perform similar operations on many data elements in parallel. A CPU is designed for a wide range of tasks including complex control flow and low-latency sequential work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>CPU strengths\u003c/h4>\u003cp>General-purpose execution, branching, interacting with operating systems, responding quickly to varied instructions, and coordinating processes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>GPU strengths\u003c/h4>\u003cp>High throughput for repetitive arithmetic applied to independent data elements, such as pixels, vectors, image filters and matrix operations.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Uses other than displaying graphics\u003c/h3>\u003cul>\u003cli>Training and using neural networks where many matrix operations can run in parallel.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Simulating fluids, weather, materials or particle interactions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Searching large numerical datasets with suitable parallel algorithms.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Processing digital images or video in bulk.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Scientific and engineering calculations that map well to parallel hardware.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Fresh example\u003c/b>\u003cp>A landscape research team must calculate a heat index independently for every cell in a large grid. A GPU can evaluate many grid cells simultaneously, while the CPU coordinates loading, scheduling and results. If each calculation depends heavily on the previous one, speed-up may be limited.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Limitations:\u003c/b> transferring data between main memory and GPU memory has a cost; branch-heavy or sequential work may make a CPU more suitable. A GPU is not simply a CPU with a higher clock speed.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"parallel","slr":2,"ref":"1.1.2(c)","title":"Multicore and parallel systems","desc":"Independent cores, parallelism, synchronisation and performance","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr02-multicore-and-parallel-systems/","html":"\n\u003cp>A \u003cb>multicore processor\u003c/b> contains two or more processing cores on one chip. A \u003cb>parallel processing system\u003c/b> uses multiple processing resources at the same time, sometimes on separate processors or separate computers. Multicore is one way to implement parallelism.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>How tasks are divided\u003c/h3>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Task parallelism:\u003c/b> different cores perform different jobs simultaneously.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Data parallelism:\u003c/b> divide a large dataset into parts and perform similar processing on each portion.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Dependencies:\u003c/b> a computation cannot always start until an earlier result exists.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overheads:\u003c/b> threads need scheduling, communication and sometimes synchronisation to avoid race conditions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Advantages\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Can reduce time for independent or divisible workloads, improve multitasking and deliver higher throughput at manageable clock speeds.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Limitations\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sequential sections, resource contention, shared-memory bottlenecks and coordination overhead prevent perfect scaling.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Processor classification enrichment\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>SISD\u003c/b>: single instruction/single data stream. \u003cb>SIMD\u003c/b>: single instruction applied to multiple data elements. \u003cb>MIMD\u003c/b>: processors execute different instructions on different data. \u003cb>MISD\u003c/b> is uncommon in practical general-purpose machines. These classifications help interpret parallel systems but are not individually named in 1.1.2(c).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Original example\u003c/b>\u003cp>A museum digitises 2,000 photographs. Independent image corrections can be divided across processor cores. A single long calculation whose next step depends on the previous result will not see the same benefit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>"},{"id":"io","slr":3,"ref":"1.1.3(a)","title":"Input and output devices","desc":"Choosing devices, sensors, ADC/DAC, RFID, actuators and displays","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr03-input-output-and-storage-devices/","html":"\n\u003cp>Input devices capture data for a computer; output devices communicate results or control physical processes. Some devices perform both functions. The OCR requirement emphasises \u003cb>choosing appropriate devices for a problem and justifying the choice\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Input methods and applications\u003c/h3>\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Device\u003c/th>\u003cth>Suitable application and reasoning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Keyboard/keypad\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Manual alphanumeric entry; compact keypads suit fixed limited choices.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Mouse/trackball/touchscreen\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pointing or direct manipulation; trackballs can require less desk space; touchscreens suit accessible kiosks.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Microphone\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sound and speech input; requires analogue-to-digital conversion unless provided digitally by the device.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2D barcode/QR reader\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quick, reliable identification of coded labels or information.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Document/3D scanner\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Digitising printed documents or measuring object geometry.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sensor\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Automatically measures a physical quantity such as temperature, gas level, pressure or light.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>RFID reader/tag\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Contactless identification; \u003cb>passive\u003c/b> tags receive energy from the reader; \u003cb>active\u003c/b> tags have their own power source and can support longer range.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch3>Output methods and applications\u003c/h3>\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Device\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strengths and considerations\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inkjet printer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Colour/photo output and lower purchase cost; consumables and print speed can be drawbacks.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Laser printer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often efficient for frequent crisp text printing; toner and initial cost should be considered.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Impact/dot-matrix printer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can print multipart forms; noisy and generally lower resolution.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3D printer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Produces physical models and specialised parts; material and print time depend on technology.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LCD / OLED display\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Visual output. LCD typically needs backlighting; OLED pixels emit their own light.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Projector\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Displays images to a larger audience; room brightness and throw distance affect results.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Speaker/headphones\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Produce sound using digital-to-analogue conversion and amplification where appropriate.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Actuator\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Converts a control signal into physical movement, such as rotating a motor or moving a valve.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch3>Monitoring compared with control\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>Monitoring\u003c/b> measures conditions and may log, display or alert without directly altering the monitored process. \u003cb>Control\u003c/b> uses feedback to change it.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\">\u003cspan>Physical quantity\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Sensor / ADC\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Processor\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>DAC / driver\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Actuator\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Many sensors produce analogue signals and require an \u003cb>ADC\u003c/b> (analogue-to-digital converter) to produce discrete readings. A control output may use a DAC or a digital driver depending on the device. Subsequent sensor readings can confirm the effect of the action.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Fresh device-selection example\u003c/b>\u003cp>A public gallery wants a low-contact visitor counter. An infrared beam sensor and controller can count interruptions and update a display. This avoids typing and manual counting, but careful positioning is needed to avoid incorrect counts when groups enter together. If the system automatically adjusts access gates, it becomes a control system rather than monitoring alone.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Justification structure:\u003c/b> name the device, identify the exact data it captures/outputs, and explain how its characteristics fit the environment, accuracy, user needs, cost or durability.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"storage","slr":3,"ref":"1.1.3(b)","title":"Secondary storage technologies","desc":"Magnetic, flash and optical storage: advantages and applications","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr03-magnetic-flash-and-optical-storage/","html":"\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Secondary storage\u003c/b> keeps programmes and data when the power is off. For 1.1.3(b), understand how \u003cstrong>magnetic, flash and optical\u003c/strong> media work, and justify when each is appropriate.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"method\">\u003ch3>Magnetic storage\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Hard disk drives (HDDs) use spinning magnetised platters, read/write heads, tracks and sectors. Magnetic tape stores data sequentially.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Advantages\u003c/b>\u003cp>Generally low cost per unit of capacity; HDDs offer large capacity and random access; tape can be economical for large archives.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Limitations\u003c/b>\u003cp>Moving parts create wear/shock concerns in HDDs; mechanical seek times add latency. Tape is slow for random retrieval.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Good applications:\u003c/b> large stationary media libraries and offline archive backups.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"method\">\u003ch3>Flash / solid-state storage\u003c/h3>\u003cp>SSD, memory cards and USB drives use electrically programmable non-volatile flash cells rather than spinning platters.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Advantages\u003c/b>\u003cp>Very fast access, no mechanical seek delay, quieter, lower mechanical-shock sensitivity and often lower power consumption.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Limitations\u003c/b>\u003cp>Typically higher cost per TB than HDDs, limited write endurance and potential data-retention concerns when powered off for very long periods.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Good applications:\u003c/b> laptop system drive, portable action camera card and fast application loading.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"method\">\u003ch3>Optical storage\u003c/h3>\u003cp>CD, DVD and Blu-ray use a laser to read changes in a disc's reflective surface. Formats may be \u003cb>read only\u003c/b>, \u003cb>recordable once\u003c/b> or \u003cb>rewritable\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Advantages\u003c/b>\u003cp>Portable removable media, low reproduction cost for some distributions, and offline transfer without a network.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Limitations\u003c/b>\u003cp>Relatively low capacity and speed by modern standards, easily scratched, and optical drives are uncommon on many new devices.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Good applications:\u003c/b> distributing a fixed disc-based collection to compatible equipment or keeping selected offline copies.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Choose using criteria, not brand names\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Consider \u003cb>capacity, access speed, throughput, portability, durability, cost per GB, energy, rewrite needs, reliability and available equipment\u003c/b>. A specific drive can have different characteristics from the typical category.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Fresh comparison\u003c/b>\u003cp>A theatre company needs a working drive for large high-resolution video edits. A fast SSD suits active editing; low-cost HDDs suit its bulk media library; a removable offline copy in a separate location supports recovery. Choosing only by advertised capacity would miss the speed and resilience requirements.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Capacities change rapidly: a CD commonly stores about 700 MB; a single-layer DVD around 4.7 GB and single-layer Blu-ray around 25 GB. Avoid treating old textbook ranges for USB or SSD as fixed modern maximums.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"memory","slr":3,"ref":"1.1.3(c)","title":"RAM and ROM","desc":"Volatility, roles and bootstrap instructions","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr03-ram-and-rom/","html":"\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Random Access Memory (RAM)\u003c/b> and \u003cb>Read Only Memory (ROM)\u003c/b> are both primary memory-related concepts but differ in their intended use and whether data persists without power.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Characteristic\u003c/th>\u003cth>RAM\u003c/th>\u003cth>ROM\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Volatility\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Normally volatile: contents lost without power\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Non-volatile: contents retained\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Main use\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Operating system, running programmes and active data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Firmware and bootstrap/start-up instructions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Read/write\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Read/write during normal operation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Read mostly; some modern firmware devices can be reprogrammed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Typical trade-off\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fast random access and flexible working space\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Persistently stores essential start-up code\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch3>What happens when a computer starts?\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Start-up firmware stored in non-volatile memory initialises hardware and begins loading the operating system from secondary storage into RAM. The CPU then executes the OS and other active software from memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>RAM compared with secondary storage\u003c/h3>\u003cp>RAM supports fast access to currently needed instructions and data. SSDs and HDDs retain files when switched off but have different latency and cost characteristics. More RAM can help workloads that otherwise run short of physical memory, but does not automatically make every programme faster.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Fresh example\u003c/b>\u003cp>A digital drawing programme loads application code and the open artwork into RAM while running. Saving the work writes it to persistent storage. Closing without saving may lose recent changes held only in volatile memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>ROM nuance\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The name “read only” describes the traditional concept; modern EEPROM/flash firmware chips can often be updated, but are still treated as non-volatile firmware storage. They are not general-purpose working RAM.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>"},{"id":"virtual","slr":3,"ref":"1.1.3(d)","title":"Virtual storage","desc":"Cloud and remote storage, benefits, risks and virtual memory distinction","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr03-virtual-storage/","html":"\n\u003cp>In OCR H446 1.1.3(d), \u003cb>virtual storage\u003c/b> means data storage presented as a convenient logical location or service even though the data may physically reside on remote servers, a network or pooled storage devices.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>How it works\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A user may save a file in a synchronised folder or remotely hosted storage. The software handles where the physical copies are stored and can provide access from multiple devices. Some services cache local copies; others require a network connection to retrieve files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Advantages\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Access files from multiple authorised devices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Facilitates sharing and collaboration.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Capacity can be increased without adding a local drive.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Providers may offer redundancy, version history and managed backup options.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Disadvantages\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Availability and speed depend on network connectivity.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Ongoing subscription, bandwidth and provider costs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Security, privacy, access control, data location and legal considerations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Provider outages and account loss can affect access.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Virtual storage is not virtual memory\u003c/h3>\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Virtual storage\u003c/th>\u003cth>Virtual memory\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A logical/remote storage service, often across networks or in the cloud.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Memory management that provides a virtual address space and may move pages between RAM and backing storage.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Used to store and access files.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Used to manage memory available to running processes.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Relevant to \u003cb>1.1.3(d)\u003c/b>.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Detailed operating-system memory management is covered in \u003cb>1.2.1(b)\u003c/b>.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cdiv class=\"example\">\u003cb>Fresh example\u003c/b>\u003cp>A robotics team stores its shared CAD files in a hosted project folder. Teammates in different buildings can access revisions. The drawback is that a disconnected workshop cannot reliably retrieve files unless they have been synchronised locally.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Exam guidance:\u003c/b> explain benefits and drawbacks in the context given. The presence of cloud storage does not guarantee a backup unless backup policies, recovery and versioning are actually provided.\u003c/p>"}],"flash":[["Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU)","Executes arithmetic, Boolean logic, comparisons and bit shifts.",1],["Control Unit (CU)","Decodes and coordinates operations and sends control signals.",1],["Register","A small, fast temporary storage location in the CPU.",1],["Programme Counter (PC)","Stores the address of the next instruction to fetch.",1],["Memory Address Register (MAR)","Stores the address of the memory location being accessed.",1],["Memory Data Register (MDR)","Holds a data item or instruction transferred to or from memory.",1],["Current Instruction Register (CIR)","Holds the instruction being decoded and executed.",1],["Accumulator (ACC)","Temporarily stores an ALU result or working value.",1],["Address bus","Carries the address identifying a memory or device location.",1],["Data bus","Carries instructions and data between processor, memory and devices.",1],["Control bus","Carries read, write, timing and other coordination signals.",1],["Opcode","The instruction field specifying what operation to perform.",1],["Operand","An instruction field specifying a value or location to use.",1],["Fetch stage","Transfers an instruction from memory into the CPU for decoding.",1],["Decode stage","Interprets an instruction's opcode and operand.",1],["Execute stage","Carries out the decoded instruction, potentially updating registers or memory.",1],["Clock speed","Number of clock cycles per second, measured in Hertz.",1],["Core","An independent processing unit within a processor.",1],["Cache","Small, fast CPU-adjacent memory for recently or commonly accessed items.",1],["Cache hit","Requested information is found in a cache level.",1],["Cache miss","Requested information is absent from a cache level and must be fetched elsewhere.",1],["Pipelining","Overlapping instruction processing stages to improve throughput.",1],["Pipeline hazard","A dependency or resource conflict that impedes ideal pipeline overlap.",1],["Von Neumann architecture","A stored-program organisation with shared instruction/data memory and path.",1],["Harvard architecture","An organisation with separate instruction and data memories/paths.",1],["Contemporary hybrid architecture","Often combines unified main memory with separate instruction/data caches.",1],["Addressability","The size of the smallest unit individually selected by a memory address.",1],["CISC","Complex Instruction Set Computer: a design with potentially complex instructions.",2],["RISC","Reduced Instruction Set Computer: typically simpler, regular instructions.",2],["Instruction set architecture (ISA)","The instructions, registers and conventions visible to compiled software.",2],["GPU","Highly parallel processor suited to many similar independent data operations.",2],["Data parallelism","Applying similar operations simultaneously to separate data items.",2],["Task parallelism","Executing different independent tasks simultaneously.",2],["Multicore processor","A single chip containing more than one execution core.",2],["Parallel processing","Using processing resources simultaneously on divisible tasks or data.",2],["Synchronisation","Coordinating concurrent operations to maintain correct access and ordering.",2],["Race condition","An error when results depend unpredictably on concurrent operation order.",2],["SISD","Single instruction stream, single data stream.",2],["SIMD","Single instruction stream applied to multiple data elements.",2],["MIMD","Multiple instruction streams operating on multiple data streams.",2],["Input device","Hardware that captures or supplies data to a system.",3],["Output device","Hardware that presents information or carries out an output action.",3],["Storage device","Hardware used to retain data and programmes.",3],["Sensor","Hardware measuring a real-world physical quantity.",3],["ADC","Analogue-to-digital converter, which samples and digitises analogue signals.",3],["DAC","Digital-to-analogue converter, which produces an analogue signal from digital values.",3],["Actuator","A component that converts a control signal into physical movement.",3],["Monitoring system","Observes and reports values without automatically controlling the process.",3],["Control system","Uses measured conditions to adjust a process through outputs.",3],["RFID","Radio frequency identification, using tags and readers for contactless identification.",3],["Passive RFID tag","A tag powered by energy from a nearby reader.",3],["Active RFID tag","A tag with its own power source for active transmission.",3],["Magnetic storage","Storage using magnetised material, such as HDD platters or tape.",3],["Flash storage","Non-volatile electronic storage using flash memory cells.",3],["Optical storage","Storage read using a laser, such as CD, DVD or Blu-ray.",3],["Solid-state drive (SSD)","A storage drive without spinning mechanical platters, normally using flash.",3],["Hard disk drive (HDD)","Magnetic storage with rotating platters and read/write heads.",3],["Random Access Memory (RAM)","Usually volatile read/write memory holding active programmes and data.",3],["Read Only Memory (ROM)","Non-volatile memory used for firmware and start-up instructions.",3],["Volatile","Data is lost when power is removed.",3],["Non-volatile","Data persists without continuous power.",3],["Firmware","Low-level software stored in non-volatile memory to control or start hardware.",3],["Virtual storage","A logical storage service often backed by remote or pooled physical devices.",3],["Virtual memory","An OS-managed virtual address space that can use paging/backing storage.",3]],"mcq":[["components","Which register points to the next instruction rather than storing the current instruction?",["CIR","PC","MDR","ACC"],1,"The PC holds the address of the next instruction to fetch."],["components","What is placed in MAR during a memory read?",["Requested memory address","Final ALU result","Clock rate","Instruction opcode only"],0,"MAR identifies the memory location being accessed."],["components","Which bus carries instructions and data in both directions?",["Address","Control only","Data","Power"],2,"The data bus normally carries transferred instructions/data."],["components","Which component performs an AND operation?",["ALU","CU","MAR","PC"],0,"Boolean logic is performed by the ALU."],["components","How many addresses are distinguishable by a 9-line address bus?",["9","256","512","1024"],2,"2^9 = 512 distinct addresses."],["components","What does CIR hold?",["The next address","The current instruction","All programme files","The cache contents"],1,"The Current Instruction Register holds the instruction being interpreted."],["components","Which bus transmits a memory-read signal?",["Data","Control","Address","Cache"],1,"A memory-read command is a control signal."],["fde","During fetch, PC's value is copied into…",["MAR","ACC","ALU","Output register"],0,"MAR receives the address of the instruction."],["fde","Which register temporarily holds the fetched instruction from RAM?",["MDR","ACC","PC","ALU"],0,"The instruction passes from memory through MDR."],["fde","What usually happens to PC during a sequential fetch?",["Becomes zero","Increments to the next address","Becomes an opcode","Copies ACC"],1,"PC normally advances to the next instruction."],["fde","What can change the PC to a non-sequential address?",["A branch instruction","A cache hit only","The screen resolution","A printer"],0,"Taken jumps override the next fetch address."],["fde","In which stage does the CU interpret the opcode?",["Fetch","Decode","Output","Maintenance"],1,"Opcode interpretation occurs during decode."],["fde","Which register normally receives a result in an accumulator-based teaching CPU?",["MAR","PC","ACC","CIR"],2,"ACC can hold arithmetic working results."],["performance","Clock speed is normally measured in…",["Hertz","Bytes","Pixels","Amperes"],0,"Hz measures clock cycles per second."],["performance","Why can cache help CPU performance?",["Reduces access to slower RAM on a cache hit","Replaces all registers","Stores files permanently","Always doubles speed"],0,"A hit supplies needed values from faster memory."],["performance","Does doubling CPU cores necessarily halve a programme's run time?",["Yes","No","Only if RAM is ROM","Only on laptops"],1,"Parallelism is limited by serial work and overhead."],["performance","Which cache is commonly the smallest and fastest?",["L1","L2","L3","Disk cache"],0,"L1 is typically nearest to the execution core and fastest."],["performance","A 6-bit byte-addressable address bus identifies how many bytes?",["6","32","64","128"],2,"2^6 = 64 addresses; each selects one byte."],["performance","What is a cache miss?",["The needed item is absent from cache","A broken processor","A failed OS","The clock stops"],0,"The CPU must access another memory level."],["pipeline","Pipelining mainly improves instruction…",["Throughput","File size","Screen resolution","Permanent storage"],0,"Overlapping stages improves instructions completed over time."],["pipeline","With ideal 3-stage pipelining, 4 instructions need how many cycles?",["4","6","9","12"],1,"Three fill stages plus three additional completions = 6 cycles."],["pipeline","A branch that changes flow can cause a…",["Control hazard","Storage allocation","ROM update","Keyboard fault"],0,"Pipeline predictions may need changing after a branch."],["pipeline","Can pipelining make one instruction require zero processing time?",["Yes","No","Only with OLED","Only on flash storage"],1,"Each instruction still passes through necessary stages."],["pipeline","A missing result from an earlier instruction can create a…",["Data hazard","Optical disc","Virtual storage mount","Firmware backup"],0,"Dependent instructions may have to wait."],["architectures","Von Neumann memory normally holds…",["Data only","Instructions only","Both instructions and data","Neither"],2,"It uses a shared main memory for instructions and data."],["architectures","What is the Von Neumann bottleneck?",["Competition for shared instruction/data access","A broken CPU fan","Full ROM","A serial port"],0,"The shared path can limit simultaneous access."],["architectures","Which design separates instructions and data memory paths?",["Harvard","Von Neumann","Sequential tape","Virtual memory"],0,"Harvard has separate paths."],["architectures","What is a common contemporary hybrid design?",["Unified RAM and split L1 instruction/data caches","No memory","Only optical storage","Two completely disconnected computers"],0,"Modern processors often combine features of both models."],["architectures","Separate instruction and data memories can permit…",["Simultaneous access","Automatic encryption","Unlimited storage","No instruction decoding"],0,"Different paths can carry both at once."],["cisc","What does CISC stand for?",["Complex Instruction Set Computer","Central Input Storage Controller","Core Instruction Signal Cache","Concurrent Instruction Storage Compiler"],0,"CISC is Complex Instruction Set Computer."],["cisc","Which philosophy often features simpler, regular load/store instructions?",["RISC","CISC only","Cloud storage","Firmware"],0,"RISC designs commonly prefer load/store and regular instructions."],["cisc","Is every RISC instruction guaranteed to complete in one cycle on real CPUs?",["Yes","No","Only on laptops","Only with 64-bit addresses"],1,"Actual timing depends on implementation and the instruction."],["cisc","Which processor family is traditionally associated with a CISC ISA?",["x86","A generic SD card","Optical disc","QR"],0,"x86 is a classic CISC example."],["cisc","What is a common advantage of RISC-like regular instruction encoding?",["Simpler decoding","No registers needed","No CPU clock","No RAM access"],0,"Regular formats can simplify decoding and pipelining."],["gpu","Which workload is most likely to suit a GPU?",["Applying the same calculation to millions of independent pixels","Typing one line","Opening a single file dialog","A single dependent scalar calculation"],0,"GPUs excel at parallel operations across many elements."],["gpu","Why might a GPU not improve a strongly sequential task?",["Later steps depend on earlier results","It has no arithmetic hardware","It cannot access memory","It only produces sound"],0,"Limited parallel work limits GPU benefit."],["gpu","Which is a non-graphics application of GPUs?",["Neural network matrix calculations","Ink cartridge refills","Printer paper feed","Mechanical disk seek"],0,"Parallel matrix operations often use GPUs."],["gpu","What can reduce GPU speed-up even when computation is parallel?",["Data transfer overhead","A better cooling fan only","Longer URLs","A physical mouse"],0,"Moving data and synchronising can be costly."],["gpu","A CPU is often better than a GPU for…",["Complex low-latency control flow","Millions of similar pixel operations","Thousands of independent matrix operations","Parallel image filters"],0,"CPUs handle varied branching and OS/control tasks well."],["parallel","A quad-core processor contains…",["Four execution cores","Four hard disks","Four screens","Four instructions total"],0,"A multicore CPU integrates several processing cores."],["parallel","Splitting independent photos across cores is…",["Data parallelism","Virtual memory","Optical storage","An instruction hazard only"],0,"Different data portions can be processed concurrently."],["parallel","What limits speed-up when part of a task cannot run in parallel?",["Sequential work","The number of keyboards","The filename extension","ROM branding"],0,"Serial sections remain a limiting factor."],["parallel","What is a race condition?",["A result depending unpredictably on timing of concurrent operations","A fast processor brand","A larger fan","A kind of printhead"],0,"Unsynchronised concurrent access can cause timing-dependent errors."],["parallel","What does SIMD mean?",["Single Instruction Multiple Data","Sequential Input Magnetic Disk","System Instruction Memory Device","Shared Internet Media Drive"],0,"SIMD applies a single instruction to multiple data elements."],["io","Which input is best for reading printed QR labels quickly?",["QR reader or camera","Speaker","Actuator","Projector"],0,"A camera/reader converts the code into data."],["io","What converts an analogue sensor signal to digital values?",["ADC","DAC","GPU","SSD"],0,"An ADC samples and digitises analogue signals."],["io","What is the role of an actuator?",["Produce physical movement from a control signal","Store permanent files","Fetch instructions","Turn light into pixels only"],0,"Actuators drive motors and other mechanisms."],["io","Which display technology has self-emitting pixels rather than a separate LCD backlight?",["OLED","LCD","Laser printer","Dot matrix"],0,"OLED pixels generate their own light."],["io","What is a passive RFID tag powered by?",["The reader's radio field","A continuously running CPU","An SSD","The screen"],0,"Passive tags harvest energy from a nearby reader."],["io","Which output is suitable for multipart impact forms?",["Dot-matrix printer","OLED","Projector","Microphone"],0,"Impact printheads can transfer marks through multipart paper."],["io","In a monitoring-only system, what happens?",["Readings may be logged or alerts sent without automatically changing the monitored process","Actuators always adjust the process","The CPU cannot run","All data is lost"],0,"Monitoring observes and reports rather than directly controlling the variable."],["storage","What technology stores data magnetically on spinning platters?",["HDD","SSD","DVD","RAM"],0,"Traditional hard disks use magnetic platters."],["storage","Which medium typically has the lowest mechanical seek latency?",["Flash SSD","HDD","Magnetic tape","Optical disc"],0,"SSDs avoid moving read heads and spinning media."],["storage","Which device normally offers sequential access and is popular for bulk archival backups?",["Magnetic tape","RAM","CPU cache","OLED"],0,"Tape is economical for some large archives but sequential to access."],["storage","What reads information from optical discs?",["Laser","Magnetic read head only","Thermal sensor","Sound waves"],0,"Optical discs are read with lasers."],["storage","Which condition is most important for a portable action camera?",["Shock resistance and write speed","A printer interface","Unlimited ROM","A shared instruction bus"],0,"Flash media are portable and tolerate vibration well."],["storage","What does CD-R usually mean?",["Recordable once","Automatically rewritten forever","RAM-backed","Requires a network"],0,"CD-R is intended for write-once recording."],["memory","Which is normally volatile?",["RAM","ROM","USB flash","Optical disc"],0,"RAM loses stored information when power is removed."],["memory","Where are active programmes and their working data usually held?",["RAM","Paper","Display pixels only","Empty DVD"],0,"The CPU uses RAM for working instructions and data."],["memory","What is a typical function of ROM/firmware?",["Hold start-up instructions","Store all unsaved user documents","Replace cache","Provide a network connection automatically"],0,"Non-volatile firmware supports system start-up."],["memory","Which device retains saved files when the computer powers down?",["SSD","Volatile RAM only","The accumulator","PC register"],0,"Secondary storage is non-volatile."],["memory","Why can more RAM help a memory-hungry application?",["It can reduce pressure on the memory manager and storage-backed paging","It changes CPU instruction set","It replaces the ALU","It automatically doubles clock rate"],0,"Additional working memory can avoid frequent slow memory transfers."],["virtual","What does virtual storage normally abstract?",["Physical location or organisation of stored files","The ALU's operation","A CPU branch opcode","The number of cores"],0,"A logical storage service hides physical placement details."],["virtual","Which is a potential drawback of remote virtual storage?",["Reliance on network access","No ability to share","Immediate local access without networking","Loss of all file types"],0,"Internet or network disruption can prevent access."],["virtual","How does virtual storage differ from virtual memory?",["Storage presents logical/remote files; virtual memory manages process address spaces","They are identical","Virtual storage is another CPU core","Virtual memory is an RFID tag"],0,"They solve different problems and appear in different specification sections."],["virtual","What should be checked before relying on cloud storage as a backup?",["Retention, versioning and recovery policy","CPU clock rate","Number of keyboard keys","Monitor brightness"],0,"A storage service does not guarantee independent backup or recovery."],["virtual","How can virtual storage support a distributed team?",["Access and collaboration on shared files","Replace all networking","Increase CPU bus width","Run all code without RAM"],0,"Remote logical storage can provide shared, authorised file access."]],"writing":[["1.1.1(a)","A teaching CPU has a 12-line address bus and uses 32-bit words, with each address selecting one word. Calculate its maximum directly addressable capacity in bytes and explain your method.","2^12 = 4096 addressable words. Each 32-bit word is 4 bytes, so total = 16,384 bytes (16 KiB). State the word-addressability assumption."],["1.1.1(a)","Explain how MAR, MDR and the data and address buses are used when a processor reads a value from memory.","The address is placed in MAR and sent via address bus. A read signal is sent. Memory returns the value on the data bus into MDR, for use in a working register or instruction."],["1.1.1(b)","A programme's next instruction is at address 73 and contains ADD 95. Describe the changes to the main registers as this instruction is fetched and decoded.","PC(73) is copied to MAR. The instruction at 73 is returned into MDR. PC normally increments to 74. MDR is copied into CIR; CU decodes ADD as opcode and 95 as a data address operand."],["1.1.1(c)","A programmer notices one application is much slower when its working data exceed cache size. Explain two reasons why adding cache might help and when it might not.","More relevant data can be supplied by fast cache instead of RAM; fewer misses reduce stalls. If access patterns have poor locality or the workload is I/O-bound, increasing cache may not help much."],["1.1.1(d)","A hypothetical three-stage pipeline processes seven independent instructions. Calculate the ideal cycle count with and without overlapping stages, and explain one source of stalls.","Without pipeline 7×3=21 cycles; with pipeline 3+7−1=9 cycles. Dependencies, branches or a shared resource can stall or flush it."],["1.1.1(e)","A manufacturer is designing a small audio filter processor and considering separate instruction/data paths. Explain a benefit and a trade-off.","Harvard separation can retrieve instructions and sample data at the same time, reducing contention; it adds complexity and may reduce flexibility in using unused memory capacity."],["1.1.2(a)","Explain why a RISC-style load/store programme might contain more instructions than an equivalent CISC-style programme without necessarily being slower.","Complex instructions can combine work into one operation; RISC may separate loads, arithmetic and stores. Simpler decoding, efficient pipelines and cycles per instruction mean instruction count alone does not determine run time."],["1.1.2(b)","A geospatial application computes the same formula for each of 4 million independent locations. Explain why a GPU may help and one reason it may not.","Many identical independent calculations map to GPU parallel units. Moving data to/from the GPU and synchronising results can add overhead, so speed-up depends on workload size and structure."],["1.1.2(c)","A team distributes independent audio-file conversions across eight cores. Identify one benefit and two limits on scaling.","Multiple conversions can run simultaneously. Limits include shared memory/storage contention, scheduling/coordination overhead and parts of the process that cannot be parallelised."],["1.1.3(a)","A large art studio needs to digitise irregular clay models to create a CAD archive. Select an input device and justify it against a keyboard-based alternative.","A 3D scanner captures surface geometry into digital form far more efficiently and accurately than manually entering coordinates; resolution, reflective surfaces and cost may need consideration."],["1.1.3(a)","A greenhouse operator only wants remote alerts when a water tank is too empty. Propose suitable input and output devices and classify the system.","A level/pressure sensor (possibly with ADC) provides readings; an alarm, display or message alerts the operator. Monitoring only, because the system does not alter water flow automatically."],["1.1.3(b)","An outdoor sound recorder must tolerate drops and record continuously while walking. Recommend a storage technology and explain two appropriate characteristics.","Flash storage, such as an SD card, lacks moving heads, is compact and consumes relatively little power. Choose a device with adequate sustained write speed and capacity."],["1.1.3(b)","A company needs economical removable storage for a monthly offline archive of very large datasets. Recommend a technology and justify the compromise.","Magnetic tape can provide high capacity and low cost for offline archival copies, though data retrieval is sequential and slower than SSD/HDD random access."],["1.1.3(c)","A student has an unsaved illustration open when the computer suddenly loses power. Explain what happens to RAM and why a saved copy might survive.","RAM is volatile and the unsaved changes held only there may be lost. A previously saved version on non-volatile SSD/HDD remains stored."],["1.1.3(d)","A charity uses shared remote project folders between offices. Give two benefits, two risks and explain why this is not the same as virtual memory.","Benefits include collaboration and location-independent access; risks include connectivity, privacy and subscription/provider dependency. Virtual storage abstracts file locations; virtual memory is OS address-space management."]],"resources":[{"name":"Craig ’n’ Dave","desc":"Free OCR H446 videos, selected to match the current topic.","url":"https://craigndave.org/ocr-a-level-h046-h446-videos/"},{"name":"Isaac Computer Science","desc":"OCR A Level topic map, explanations and practice.","url":"https://isaaccomputerscience.org/topics/a_level?examBoard=ocr&stage=a_level#ocr"},{"name":"Physics & Maths Tutor","desc":"OCR A Level 1.1 notes, flashcards and practice links.","url":"https://www.physicsandmathstutor.com/computer-science-revision/a-level-ocr/characteristics-of-contemporary-processors"},{"name":"CompSci Wizard","desc":"OCR H446 topic notes, flashcards and questions.","url":"https://compsciwizard.com/topics"}]};
